<ecoSpold xmlns="http://www.EcoInvent.org/EcoSpold02">
  <activityDataset>
    <activityDescription>
      <activity id="e8952050-cc2d-5370-8e9d-4817debf0afd" activityNameId="c9058caa-9135-5675-979a-858f33bc6038" activityNameContextId="f44c43c1-5cc5-54a6-ade6-24435bd12c83" type="1" specialActivityType="0" energyValues="0">
        <activityName xml:lang="en">Nuclear spent fuel reprocessing plant</activityName>
        <generalComment>
          <text xml:lang="en" index="1">Full activity name: Nuclear spent fuel reprocessing plant
Data on material and energy for construction are relatively poor, sometimes the only available were from non-realised projects (in USA and DE). The THORP plant (BNFL, Sellafield, UK) and its adjoint facilities (for vitrification and conditioning of waste from light water reactors) have been used as main reference for the estimation of the infrastructure, although for the operation most of the data are for UP2 and UP3 plants (Cogema, La Hague, FR). Annual design throughput is 1200 tonne heavy metal. Extrapolations have been made to take into account visual estimations and aerial photos of the facilities. No direct emissions during construction are considered, only indirect through the energy (and material) requirements. Decommissioning is only very roughly described. Only non-radioactive wastes from dismantling are accounded for, because low radioactive wastes are attributed to operation.;
UUID: 1582dc6e-e029-3b4e-9c8e-1c0ab1a0053e
The module includes an estimation of the main material and energy use for the construction and the decommissioning of the facility. Land use, transport requirements, excavation for foundation, and disposal of inerts from decommissioning of the plant are also accounted for. 
Location: European facilities.
Technology: Current commercial facilities.
Time period: undefined
Production volume: na
Sampling: Literature 
Extrapolations: none
Uncertainty adjustments: none
BAFU mapped export: unsupported biosphere exchanges excluded; see audit/. Local UUIDs and generic activity classification are export conventions. Source: process_1582dc6e-e029-3b4e-9c8e-1c0ab1a0053e.xml</text>
        </generalComment>
      </activity>
